<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Lyttos</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.) · capital Lyttos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Inland city of central Crete claiming Spartan descent, destroyed by Knossos in the Lyttian War of 220 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lyttos lay on a high plateau of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/knossos">Knossos</a>, and was one of the oldest cities on the island; it appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>. Its people claimed to be colonists from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a>, and ancient writers saw its customs as close to Spartan ones.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> cites Lyttos as an example of the Cretan constitution with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosmoi">kosmoi</a>, a council of elders and common messes. It controlled harbours on the north coast, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chersonesus-taurica">Chersonesos</a>. Lyttos was a staunch enemy of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/knossos">Knossos</a> and fought it repeatedly for leadership of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the Lyttian War of 220 BCE Knossian forces destroyed Lyttos while its army was away. The city was rebuilt and resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-creticus">Metellus</a> took it during the conquest of 69 to 67 BCE. It continued as a Roman town.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lyttos.jpg" alt="Lyttos" width="320" height="430"><figcaption>Image: AlMare · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traianus_Lyttos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lyttos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cretan Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lyctus</dd></dl></aside></div>

